{"nodes": [{"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_tracing_tracing_panel", "label": "DevTools tracing panel", "file_type": "document", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/tracing.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_tracing_trace_list", "label": "Trace list", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/tracing.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "One row per trace with route, message, payload and duration. A message name is its kind when it has one, which is how this codebase tags its unions - constructor.name alone answers Object for every object literal and tells you nothing."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_tracing_flame_graph_and_waterfall", "label": "Flame graph and waterfall views", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/tracing.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "They coincide for a straight chain and separate when an actor sends to several others: the flame graph puts the siblings on one row, the waterfall gives each its own. Timings come from a monotonic clock and are shown in microseconds, because actor message handling routinely completes inside a millisecond and wall-clock timestamps would draw every bar as zero-width."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_tracing_teetracer", "label": "TeeTracer", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/tracing.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Attaching DevTools does not take your tracer away: if nothing is installed it enables a recorder, and if something already is it wraps it so both consumers see every span. Detaching puts the original back exactly as it was, which is what lets the panel run against a service that is also exporting traces for real."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_tracing_span_buffer", "label": "Span buffer", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/tracing.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A bounded ring held on the server as well as in the panel, so a browser that connects late still gets it, flushed to the browser in batches rather than one span at a time because a busy system would otherwise turn a debugging aid into a firehose. On overflow the oldest spans are dropped and the panel says how many - a flame graph is about the recent past, so that is the half worth keeping."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_tracing_trace_context_requirement", "label": "Actors trace only messages that already belong to a trace", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/tracing.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A cell opens an actor.receive span when the incoming message carries a trace context or a span is already active, and nothing in the framework starts the first one. So a plain tell records nothing and a system that is plainly busy can leave the panel empty; seed a span at your entry point and every tell inside it carries the context onwards."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_tracing_tracerof", "label": "tracerOf", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/tracing.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Returns the tracer installed right now. Held in a variable from before DevTools.attach it is still the noop, whose withActiveSpan runs your callback and records nothing, silently - so call it at the point of use."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_tracing_devtools_actors_excluded_from_traces", "label": "DevTools actors excluded from traces", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/tracing.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Never a root span and never a child one either: its probes receive event-stream publishes during an application message and would otherwise reappear in the middle of that message route. Without the internal marking the hub publishing the spans it just recorded would feed every batch back in as the payload of the next one."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_tracing_tracing_always_recording", "label": "Tracing records from attach, with no button", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/tracing.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The messages worth looking at are the ones that already went past, so tracing runs from the moment DevTools attaches and the recent history is there when you open the panel. Expect volume to match traffic: it opens a root span for every message and serialises each payload - that is the trade DevTools.attach makes, and a system that cannot afford it should not have a debugger attached to it."}], "edges": [{"source": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_tracing_tracing_panel", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_tracing_trace_list", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/tracing.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_tracing_tracing_panel", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_tracing_flame_graph_and_waterfall", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/tracing.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_tracing_tracing_panel", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_tracing_teetracer", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/tracing.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_tracing_tracing_panel", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_tracing_span_buffer", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/tracing.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_tracing_tracing_panel", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_tracing_trace_context_requirement", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/tracing.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_tracing_tracing_panel", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_tracing_tracerof", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/tracing.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_tracing_tracing_panel", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_tracing_devtools_actors_excluded_from_traces", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACT